--TEST--
`Core\Process\Result` hands back both captures whatever the child exited with — over a sweep of statuses each run's `stdout`, `stderr` and `exitCode` arrive together, so a result that kept output only on success fails by count — `rule:core-classes/process-run`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `process-a-completed-run-answers-the-status-the-child-chose.nvst` asks what
// the status is, over a child that writes nothing;
// `process-a-completed-runs-two-streams-stay-apart.nvst` asks what the two
// streams carry, over a child that exits `0`. Neither can see the third
// question, which is whether the three answers belong to the *same* run: a
// member that captured only what a successful child wrote, or that answered
// the status of one run beside the output of another, passes both of them.
//
// So each row below asserts all three at once and the counts are over the whole
// sweep. `0` is in it as the control — a result that keeps output on success
// alone still prints `kept beside its code=1` here — and `255` is the last
// status a shell can carry, so the sweep is bounded at the end a `run` that
// widened a status or read one byte of it would fail at.
//
// The markers are compared as `bytes` and by containment, for the reasons
// `process-a-completed-runs-two-streams-stay-apart.nvst` gives: the octets a
// child writes are not this program's to assume anything about, and each shell
// ends a line its own way and one of them pads before a redirect.
//
// The two candidates are the platform shells named absolutely, for the reason
// the status case states: § 1 makes a `PATH` lookup the caller's own, and the
// one that is not this platform's starts nothing — `IOError` on Windows,
// `RuntimeError` for a path with no root on Unix.

array<int> $statuses = [0, 1, 2, 42, 255];

int $started = 0;
int $agreed = 0;
int $keptOut = 0;
int $keptErr = 0;
int $together = 0;

foreach ($statuses as int $status) {
    // `-2` and not `-1` for the reason the status case gives: a signalled child
    // answers `-1`, so a sentinel spelled that way could not be told apart from
    // a real status.
    int $code = -2;
    bytes $out = "" as bytes;
    bytes $err = "" as bytes;

    try {
        var $unix = Core\Process::run("/bin/sh", [
            "-c",
            Core\Str::format("printf OUT; printf ERR 1>&2; exit %d", $status)
        ]);
        $started = $started + 1;
        $code = $unix->exitCode();
        $out = $unix->stdout();
        $err = $unix->stderr();
    } catch (IOError $notAUnix) {
    }

    try {
        var $windows = Core\Process::run("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", [
            "/c",
            Core\Str::format("echo OUT& echo ERR 1>&2& exit %d", $status)
        ]);
        $started = $started + 1;
        $code = $windows->exitCode();
        $out = $windows->stdout();
        $err = $windows->stderr();
    } catch (RuntimeError $notWindows) {
    }

    bool $sameCode = $code == $status;
    bool $hasOut = Core\Bytes::contains($out, "OUT" as bytes);
    bool $hasErr = Core\Bytes::contains($err, "ERR" as bytes);

    if ($sameCode) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
    if ($hasOut) { $keptOut = $keptOut + 1; }
    if ($hasErr) { $keptErr = $keptErr + 1; }
    if ($sameCode && $hasOut && $hasErr) { $together = $together + 1; }
}

echo "statuses=", Core\Arr::count($statuses), "\n";
echo "started=", $started, "\n";
echo "codes agreed=", $agreed, "\n";
echo "stdout kept=", $keptOut, "\n";
echo "stderr kept=", $keptErr, "\n";
echo "kept beside its code=", $together, "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `true` for the reason the exit-code case gives: the door's own scoping is
# pinned by
# `process-run-asks-for-the-capability-before-it-looks-at-the-target.nvst`, and
# this case is about what a completed run answers.
[capabilities.process]
exec = true
--EXPECT--
statuses=5
started=5
codes agreed=5
stdout kept=5
stderr kept=5
kept beside its code=5
